2026-07-30 09:29:00,142 - INFO - main.py:197 - FRONTEND DEBUG: {"level": "log", "message": "fetchQuestions: Received response data: {\"total_count\":1546,\"questions\":[{\"question_id\":1553,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-07-30\",\"question_text\":\"A Year 12 student is preparing a scientific presentation to explain the forces acting on a passenger of mass \\\\(m\\\\) standing on a weighing scale inside an elevator that is accelerating upwards with an acceleration \\\\(a\\\\). Taking the upward direction as positive and standard gravity as \\\\(g\\\\), which of the following statements correctly communicates the magnitude of the normal force \\\\(F_N\\\\) exerted by the scale and the underlying reasoning using Newton's laws?\",\"choice_1\":\"\\\\(F_N = m(g + a)\\\\); because the normal force must overcome gravity and provide the net force required for upward acceleration.\",\"choice_2\":\"\\\\(F_N = m(g - a)\\\\); because upward acceleration reduces the effective gravitational pull on the passenger.\",\"choice_3\":\"\\\\(F_N = m(a - g)\\\\); because the net force acting on the object is the difference between acceleration and gravity.\",\"choice_4\":\"\\\\(F_N = mg\\\\); because by Newton's Third Law, the normal force is always equal in magnitude to the force of gravity.\",\"correct_option\":\"A\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"By applying Newton's Second Law (\\\\(\\\\sum F_y = ma_y\\\\)) along the upward positive axis:\\n\\n\\\\(F_N - F_g = ma\\\\)\\n\\nSince \\\\(F_g = mg\\\\), substituting gives:\\n\\n\\\\(F_N - mg = ma \\\\implies F_N = m(g + a)\\\\)\\n\\nTo communicate this scientific concept clearly and accurately, one must state that the normal force (scale reading) increases beyond the static weight because it must both counteract the downward pull of gravity and supply the net force necessary for upward acceleration.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1552,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-07-29\",\"question_text\":\"\u0623\u064a\u0651 \u0645\u0646 \u0627\u0644\u0639\u0628\u0627\u0631\u0627\u062a \u0627\u0644\u062a\u0627\u0644\u064a\u0629 \u062a\u064f\u0639\u0628\u0651\u0631 \u0628\u0634\u0643\u0644 \u0635\u062d\u064a\u062d \u0648\u062f\u0642\u064a\u0642 \u0639\u0646 \\\"\u0642\u0627\u0646\u0648\u0646 \u0646\u064a\u0648\u062a\u0646 \u0627\u0644\u0623\u0648\u0644\\\" (\u0645\u0628\u062f\u0623 \u0627\u0644\u0639\u0637\u0627\u0644\u0629)\u061f\",\"choice_1\":\"\u064a\u062d\u0627\u0641\u0638 \u0643\u0644 \u062c\u0633\u0645 \u0639\u0644\u0649 \u0633\u0643\u0648\u0646\u0647 \u0623\u0648 \u062d\u0631\u0643\u062a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0645\u0633\u062a\u0642\u064a\u0645\u0629 \u0627\u0644\u0645\u0646\u062a\u0638\u0645\u0629 \u0625\u0630\u0627 \u0644\u0645 \u062a\u0624\u062b\u0631 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0642\u0648\u0629 \u062e\u0627\u0631\u062c\u064a\u0629 \u062a\u063a\u064a\u0631 \u0645\u0646 \u062d\u0627\u0644\u062a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u062d\u0631\u0643\u064a\u0629.\",\"choice_2\":\"\u062a\u0633\u0627\u0631\u0639 \u0627\u0644\u062c\u0633\u0645 \u064a\u062a\u0646\u0627\u0633\u0628 \u0637\u0631\u062f\u064a\u0627\u064b \u0645\u0639 \u0645\u062d\u0635\u0644\u0629 \u0627\u0644\u0642\u0648\u0649 \u0627\u0644\u0645\u0624\u062b\u0631\u0629 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0648\u0639\u0643\u0633\u064a\u0627\u064b \u0645\u0639 \u0643\u062a\u0644\u062a\u0647.\",\"choice_3\":\"\u0644\u0643\u0644 \u0642\u0648\u0629 \u0641\u0639\u0644 \u0642\u0648\u0629 \u0631\u062f \u0641\u0639\u0644 \u0645\u0633\u0627\u0648\u064a\